      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Progesterone is critical for maintaining pregnancy and onset of labor. We evaluated CYP450-mediated progesterone meta-bolism, specifically the contribution of CYP3A isoforms."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "In vitro progesterone metabolism was characterized in human liver microsomes (HLMs) with and without selective cytochrome P450 inhibitors and in recombinant CYP3A4, CYP3A5, and CYP3A7. 6β-hydroxyprogesterone (6β-OHP) and 16α-hydroxyprogesterone (16α-OHP) metabolites were quantified by HPLC/UV and fit to the Michaelis-Menten equation to determine Km and Vmax. The effect of CYP3A5 expression on progesterone clearance was determined by in vitro in vivo extrapolation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Ketoconazole inhibited formation of both 6β-OHP and 16α-OHP more than 95%. 6β-OHP and 16α-OHP were both produced by CYP3A4 (2.3 and 1.3 µL/min/pmol, respectively) to a greater extent than by CYP3A5 (0.09 and 0.003 µL/min/pmol) and CYP3A7 (0.004 and 0.003 µL/min/pmol)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Maternal clearance of progesterone by hepatic CYP450's is driven primarily by CYP3A4, with limited contributions from CYP3A5 and CYP3A7."
        }
      ]},
